## Context

0943 ported `skills/reviewers/reviewers.sh:_keystore_value` into
`skills/external-peer-review/peer_review.py` and, answering its review panel,
fixed a defect the reference still carries.

`. "$1" >/dev/null 2>&1 || exit 3` reports the status of the LAST command the
sourced file ran, not whether sourcing succeeded. A provider file whose final
line exits non-zero therefore makes a correctly-defined variable look
unreadable -- and in `reviewers.sh` that is a seat that cannot authenticate,
which is the fail-open class `feedback_gaze_external_seats_fail_open` and 0393
exist to prevent. `peer_review.py` now probes `[ -r "$1" ]` instead, which asks
the question actually being asked; a genuine mid-file failure still leaves the
name unset and exits 4.

Two smaller items from the same panel belong with it, since they share the
idiom and would otherwise be fixed twice:

- An explicit `exit N` inside a provider file still loses an already-set
  credential, in both copies.
- A CRLF provider file leaves a trailing carriage return inside the value: a
  byte-wrong credential that looks present and fails only at the API call.
  `peer_review.py` strips it; `reviewers.sh` does not.

The two copies diverging is the drift 0943's own design notes warned about, so
this is convergence work, not a new feature.

## Exit criteria
- [ ] `reviewers.sh:_keystore_value` probes readability rather than reading the
      sourced file's last exit status
- [ ] A trailing carriage return is stripped from the extracted value
- [ ] A shell test covers a provider file ending on a failing command and one
      written with CRLF, both with fake sentinels and never a real credential
- [ ] The comment in `peer_review.py` that records the divergence is updated or
      removed once the two agree
- [ ] `make check` passes
